# The self-hosted form's settings, read by docker-compose.yml (README, "Self-hosting"). Copy to
# `.env` beside it, then fill the three groups below. Everything here also reaches the graft
# container, so any server variable documented in packages/env/src/schema.ts may be added.

# 1. Secrets that Graft refuses to start without, and the admin's password beside them. Mint
#    all six at once with the image itself:
#      docker compose run --rm --no-deps graft node dist/keys.mjs >> .env
#    (or, from a checkout with pnpm: pnpm --filter @graft/server keys >> .env)
#    Nothing in this file or in docker-compose.yml supplies a value for any of them: a default
#    would be a secret in a public repository (GRA-148).
# GRAFT_AUTH_SECRET=
# GRAFT_KEYRING_SECRET=
# GRAFT_HANDOFF_SECRET=
# GRAFT_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# GRAFT_CAPABILITY_TOKEN_PRIVATE_KEY=
# GRAFT_CAPABILITY_TOKEN_PUBLIC_KEY=

# 2. The model that authors tools (ADR 0014: a self-host brings its own key). The provider group and
#    the rule that a production self-host refuses to start without it are GRA-31's. The provider is
#    `anthropic` or `openai`; the two model ids are optional and default per provider (for openai
#    the two shown; for anthropic claude-fable-5-1 and claude-haiku-4-5-20251001); the base URL
#    points the openai provider at an OpenAI-compatible gateway.
# GRAFT_MODEL_BACKEND=provider
# GRAFT_MODEL_PROVIDER=openai
# GRAFT_MODEL_API_KEY=
# GRAFT_MODEL_AUTHORING=gpt-5.6-sol
# GRAFT_MODEL_TRIAGE=gpt-5.4-mini
# GRAFT_MODEL_BASE_URL=

# Optional: route the vendors behind your API gateway through it (ADR 0019, GRA-58). A vendor whose
#    hosts are all listed connects with no person step and every call relays to the upstream with the
#    identity header attached, the vendor URL in the path: <upstream>/<vendor host>/<vendor path>.
#    The four are all-or-nothing; the prefix is optional and only read beside them. https in production.
# GRAFT_GATEWAY_HOSTS=api.vendor.example,*.googleapis.com
# GRAFT_GATEWAY_UPSTREAM_URL=https://gateway.corp.example/graft
# GRAFT_GATEWAY_HEADER_NAME=X-Deployment-Token
# GRAFT_GATEWAY_HEADER_VALUE=
# GRAFT_GATEWAY_HEADER_PREFIX=

# Optional: sign in with a Google or a GitHub account beside email and password (GRA-81, ADR 0020).
# Each pair is all-or-nothing and off by default; the console shows a button per configured
# provider. Register the client with the vendor using the redirect URI
# <GRAFT_PUBLIC_URL>/api/auth/callback/google or …/callback/github.
# GRAFT_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GRAFT_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GRAFT_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# GRAFT_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Optional: send the password-reset email through your own SMTP relay (ADR 0021, GRA-92) — a mailbox
# provider's, SES's SMTP endpoint, a company mail server. The pair is all-or-nothing; the URL carries
# the credentials. Unset, the reset link is printed in `docker compose logs graft` instead.
# GRAFT_SMTP_URL=smtps://user:password@smtp.example.com:465
# GRAFT_MAIL_FROM=Graft <no-reply@example.com>

# Optional: the chat products whose connectors may answer Graft's in-chat ask card (GRA-84, ADR 0006
# as amended 2026-09-21), by the host their OAuth callback is on. This is the whole rule: a
# connector registered on any other host answers every ask in the console instead, whatever it
# claims about itself when it connects. The default is Claude and ChatGPT; add a host only for a
# chat product you run yourself, and only if it hides Graft's app-only tools from its model.
# GRAFT_CARD_HOSTS=claude.ai,chatgpt.com

# Optional: rate limits, one door at a time (GRA-149). Graft is unlimited by default and every one
# of these is off until you set it; each is <limit>/<windowSeconds>, counted in this process, so
# two replicas hold two counts. Set the ones you want and leave the rest out. The numbers below are
# a starting point for a self-host, not a recommendation: sign-in and OAuth registration are keyed
# on the caller's address and so is MCP, the proxy on the connection, the API on the person.
# GRAFT_RATE_LIMIT_SIGN_IN=20/60
# GRAFT_RATE_LIMIT_OAUTH_REGISTER=10/60
# GRAFT_RATE_LIMIT_OAUTH_TOKEN=60/60
# GRAFT_RATE_LIMIT_MCP=600/60
# GRAFT_RATE_LIMIT_PROXY=600/60
# GRAFT_RATE_LIMIT_API=300/60
# Behind a reverse proxy or a load balancer, how many hops are in front: the caller's address is
# then that many entries from the right of X-Forwarded-For. Unset, the header is never read and the
# address is the socket's, because anyone may send that header.
# GRAFT_TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1

# 3. The admin opened on first start, into an empty database only. The pair is all-or-nothing:
#    put your address here, and let the keys script in group 1 write GRAFT_ADMIN_PASSWORD. A
#    password shorter than 16 characters, or the one this repository shipped until GRA-148, is
#    refused at boot with a sentence naming the variable. Leave both unset to register through
#    the console's /signup instead; the verification link is in `docker compose logs graft`.
GRAFT_ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com

# Where the console and the API answer, as the browser sees them. Behind a domain, the https origin.
GRAFT_PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000
# The host port the console and the API answer on.
GRAFT_PORT=3000
# Postgres is not published by default. `pnpm run db:start` adds docker-compose.dev.yml, which
# publishes it on loopback for a server run from source; this is the port it uses there.
GRAFT_POSTGRES_PORT=5432
# The Docker socket's group inside the graft container: 0 under Docker Desktop, on Linux the
# output of `stat -c %g /var/run/docker.sock`.
GRAFT_DOCKER_GID=0
# Which published images to run; a local build tags itself with the same name.
GRAFT_IMAGE_TAG=latest
